cs and  customer preferences and so on  so um as you may be aware in kaggle  often the winning solution is um  using xgb boost or light gpm in some  capacity  so actually kaggle is really where these  frameworks  made the name for themselves  and what we see here is that on all  three of these use cases  um when we compare the  test metric whether it's log loss or  root mean squared error for the  progression problems  we see that snapboost um is beating xgb  boost in like gpm  and i'd like to emphasize that this is  after  hyperparameter tuning so um  this is the tuned uh test loss  for these three different frameworks  so again uh this is our most recent  research and we just  um had our paper on snapboost accepted  to the eurips conference which will take  place  in december so please check out that  paper for more details  next job please okay so finally this is  um  a real industrial application of  snapboost this was a  ibm customer in the financial services  domain who came to  us saying that they have a gradient  boosting workload  they were actually using a different  framework they were using the  gbm from h2o that's about all we know  they wouldn't tell us the data set or  the learning task  and they ran all of the experiments  themselves and this is what they  reported back to us  basically they compared the h2o boosting  machine  excuse me  they compare the h2o boosting machine  after extensive  hyper parameter tuning with the  snapboost  algorithm again after extensive  hyperparameter tuning  are both here running on the gpu  and they found that snapboost could give  them 12  improved accuracy and the end to end run  time was very similar so the total time  it took them to do the tuning um  was around 20 minutes in both cases  okay next slide please  okay so now we're going to talk about  the  the inference acceleration aspect of  snapmall which is again  um some of our very recent work  so the goal with inference acceleration  is  of course primarily 